  "N42" [label="(34,0,0) Self-complementary DONs result in overall loss of structural control beyond the repetitive subunit"];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) If a method loses structural control beyond subunits, then it cannot maintain site-specific addressability across the entire superstructure"];
  "N57" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given current approaches show opposing trade-offs between cost and control, and such situations indicate a gap, therefore concepts combining low cost and high structural versatility are missing"];
  "N58" [label="(40,0,0) Concepts combining low cost and high structural versatility could alleviate issues with traditional hierarchical assembly and enable controlled assembly of large superstructures with retained site-specific addressability"];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) If modularity can be engineered into connection methods, then a single structure could generate many configurations without requiring many unique components"];
  "N64" [label="(46,0,0) A multitude of connection sites was achieved by engineering a re-routable scaffold"];
  "N65" [label="(0,0,0) If scaffold routing can be made re-routable instead of fixed, then one scaffold can create multiple different connection configurations"];
  "N78" [label="(42,0,0) The large number of orthogonal connection sites allows increasing monomers in one-pot assemblies, circumventing extraction and purification of intermediates required in traditional hierarchical assembly"];
  "N79" [label="(55,0,0) Superstructures were assembled in one-step reactions from monomers without purification/isolation of intermediates"];
  "N81" [label="(0,0,0) If orthogonal connections enable one-pot assembly, then intermediate purification steps can be eliminated, simplifying construction and increasing yield"];
  "N84" [label="(58,0,0) Site-specific addressability of each monomer in superstructures is fully retained, demonstrated by placing gold nanoparticles at specific positions"];
  "N85" [label="(0,0,0) If a modular system retains site-specific addressability across superstructures, then it combines the advantages of both cost-effectiveness and structural control"];
  "N93" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given moDON demonstrates multiple assembly types with controllable processes and such demonstrations indicate high versatility, therefore moDON provides the high structural versatility that was missing"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study develops and evaluates a modular DNA origami monomer, moDON, whose re-routable scaffold generates tens of thousands of shape-matching xy interfaces and whose orthogonal three-strand z connectors enable one-pot programmable assembly, selective disassembly, retained addressability, arbitrary 3D superstructures, and gigadalton-scale periodic architectures.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A DNA origami platform that combines modular xy interfaces, orthogonal z connectors, one-pot assembly, retained addressability, controllable disassembly, and validated finite and periodic 3D structures addresses limitations of prior size-increase and hierarchical assembly strategies."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) A re-routable scaffold can generate multiple different connection configurations, supporting modular xy interface variation."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) SST assembly’s need for individually designed and synthesized ssDNA tiles indicates a many-component burden with scalability and cost challenges."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) DNA origami slat assembly is limited for 3D assembly needs because it is restricted to planar 2D structures with low complexity."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) ssDNA connector construction has practical limitations because requiring many unique connectors per connection makes unwanted interactions and incomplete connections more likely."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Self-complementary DONs lose structural control beyond repetitive subunits and therefore cannot maintain site-specific addressability across entire superstructures."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) The observed trade-offs motivate DNA origami systems that combine low cost, high structural versatility, and retained site-specific addressability."];
  "R31" [label="(0,0,0) Engineering a re-routable scaffold supports many shape-matching connection-site variants for moDON monomers."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) Orthogonal connection sites enable one-pot moDON assembly by allowing more monomers to assemble without extraction and purification of intermediates."];
  "R36" [label="(0,0,0) One-step moDON assembly without purification or isolation of intermediates simplifies construction and can increase overall yield."];
  "R37" [label="(0,0,0) moDON retains site-specific addressability in superstructures, as demonstrated by placement of gold nanoparticles at specified positions."];
  "R38" [label="(0,0,0) moDON’s retained site-specific addressability supports combining cost-effectiveness with structural control in modular superstructures."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) DNA origami scaffold-size constraints motivate strategies to increase DNA origami nanostructure sizes."];
  "R40" [label="(0,0,0) moDON’s modular connection methods plausibly provide a versatile basis for generating many configurations without requiring many unique components."];
  "R41" [label="(0,0,0) Developing modular connection methods in a single moDON structure is a plausible way to address the gap in low-cost, versatile, addressable DNA origami assembly."];
